8 #edb/ref 17592186314934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096622 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186314935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096622 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096622 #edb/ref 1039 "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096622 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186096422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096622 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186097435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1045 "The Quotient Rule for Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186097430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1050 900.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186096890 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a699a755-f56a-5a6a-8493-60b83f42eaa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097430 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097430 #edb/ref 1061 12332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097430 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ed5427f5-4392-5166-a825-dd9dcb07cc3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097430 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097431 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097431 #edb/ref 1061 12333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097431 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6859d258-9c69-5bab-88e5-4b946166e00d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097431 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097432 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097432 #edb/ref 1061 12334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097432 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f384d7ca-2c43-5aae-b786-23e8a903bb4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097432 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097433 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097433 #edb/ref 1061 12335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097433 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "185d55a4-49d5-53d5-95b3-8eb93729ad7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097433 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097434 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186097435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097434 #edb/ref 1061 12336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097434 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f7097f26-3f43-5e1d-8496-783599f1b7ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097434 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186096622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097435 #edb/ref 1061 12337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097435 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c001eb53-21b0-5423-a50f-1e506d151f8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097435 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186097822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097820 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to calculate the derivative of the product of two functions, but what if we have a quotient?\n\nFor that, we can use the **quotient rule**. The quotient rule is a formula to calculate the derivative of the ratio of two functions like \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {u(x)}{v(x)}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule for differentiation states that the derivative of the function above is given by\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac {u'(x)\\cdot v(x) - u(x) \\cdot v'(x)} {[v(x)]^2}.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that we have the function $f(x)$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{x}{x^2+1},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $f'(x).$ \n\nHere $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=x^2+1.$  Their derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{'}(x) & =(x)^{'}=1, \\\\ {v}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2}+1)^{'}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug them in the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097820 #edb/ref 1065 2624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097820 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dd2362a7-73d2-577f-aa31-2150f8f6b63f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097820 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097821 #edb/ref 1064 "Using Leibniz notation, the quotient rule is expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left (\\dfrac u v\\right) = \\dfrac {v \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}u} {\\textrm{d}x}\n- u \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x}}{v^2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097821 #edb/ref 1065 2625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097821 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4ed29747-9007-50b8-9347-89e46eabc182" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097821 #edb/ref 1067 "Leibniz Notation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097822 #edb/ref 1064 "We can now prove that $(\\tan x)' = \\sec^2{x}$ using the quotient rule. Here's how! \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}x)-(\\operatorname{sin}x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}x+{sin}^{2}x}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097822 #edb/ref 1065 2626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097822 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "584707ea-2c96-5389-926d-b9ac87adc17c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186097822 #edb/ref 1067 "Justifying the Formula for the Derivative of Tangent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234507 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234507 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234507 #edb/ref 1071 "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234507 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e9ec61c-a65c-56b3-820e-2d60ba70912c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186234507 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314003 #edb/ref 1080 "1-2\\operatorname{ln}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314003 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314003 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe403dd4-e8fc-5b37-a657-5cdb8ad41fe7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314167 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186314003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314167 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314167 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314167 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186314003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314167 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "392caec0-4bec-552a-a683-fe5f52bda24d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314935 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314935 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314935 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314935 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{{x}^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ac3d430-ffd8-5d49-8cb6-f41434a00666" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186314935 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $u={log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}$ and $v={x}^{2}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{xln⁡3}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡3}-\\frac{ln⁡x}{ln⁡3}\\cdot 2x)}{({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{x}{ln⁡3}-\\frac{2xln⁡x}{ln⁡3})}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2xln⁡x}{{x}^{4}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(1-2ln⁡x)}{{x}^{4}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2ln⁡x}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3} \\\\  & =(\\,1-2ln⁡x\\,)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}ln⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428105 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428105 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [2]; waits [2]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1110->280. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428105 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050420 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186488408 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488446 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488408 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488408 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x)=\\frac{x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{e^{x} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the denominator is nonzero." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488408 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d6589dd-899d-5e9f-b37f-cdd937732bad" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186488408 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=x^{2} + \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $v(x)=e^{x} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime